#!/usr/bin/env bash
# fusion-work-order — the order the work-item store imposes on itself.
#
# Usage:
#   fusion-work-order
#
# Takes no arguments. Output on stdout, one KEY=value per line, then one row per
# item in the computed order, prerequisites first, then the cycle and unresolved
# rows:
#
#   anchor=workbench-root
#   items=7
#   edges=5
#   unresolved-edges=2
#   cycles=1
#   ready=3
#   roots=3
#   no-depends-on-field=4
#   unreadable-head=1
#   verdict=cyclic
#   note=4 items carry no `**Depends-on:**` field …
#      1      0       2  ready    <item-a>
#      2      0       1  paused   <item-f>
#      3      1       0  blocked  <item-b>
#   cycle=<item-c>, <item-d>
#   unresolved=<item-b> wants <item-e>.md
#   unreadable=<item-g>
#
# The item row is five fixed columns — order, depth, blocks, readiness,
# container name. The readiness column reads `ready`, `blocked` or `paused`: the
# third is the item's own `**Status:**` and overrides the other two whatever its
# out-edges, because `ready` invites a reader to pick the item up and a paused
# item is one somebody has set down. Its `depth` and `blocks` stay computed like
# any node's. `ready=` counts the items with no RESOLVED unmet prerequisite and
# `roots=` the items at depth 0, where the order starts; a paused item never
# counts in `ready=` and still counts at depth 0, so the two are equal in an
# acyclic store with no paused item in it and differ where a cycle sits at
# depth 0 or where a paused item does.
#
# `ready=` is optimistic by up to two counts: `no-depends-on-field=` (an absent
# field asserts nothing) and `unresolved-edges=` (an entry naming live work in a
# form the grammar does not define blocks nothing here; only an entry naming a
# terminal item is genuinely no edge). Neither is measurable from here.
#
# A node is a work item whose `**Status:**` is `open`, `claimed` or `paused`. A
# `done` or `dropped` item is outside the graph, its outgoing entries unread,
# and an entry naming one is reported as unresolved like an entry naming
# nothing at all. An item-form record whose head yields no readable
# `**Status:**` is outside for a different reason, a failed parse, and is
# counted in `unreadable-head=` and named on an `unreadable=` row rather than
# dropped in silence.
# `archive/**` is never opened. The computation is hooks/lib/work-graph.ts and
# its header is the authoritative account of all of that.
#
# `note=` IS MANDATORY WHENEVER `no-depends-on-field=` OR `unresolved-edges=` IS
# ABOVE ZERO, and it is a user's ruling rather than a courtesy. The field is
# permitted rather than mandated and is absent when there is nothing to say, so
# an absent field and a genuinely prerequisite-free item are indistinguishable;
# the con was accepted at the gate on the condition that this program say so
# rather than leave a reader to infer it
# (`260908-2018_*_does-the-record-template-mandate-the-prerequisite-field-or-merely-permit-it.md`,
# the `Answered:` line). The unresolved half joined on the same reasoning. The
# line kind is bin/fusion-forum's: a degradation that changed the answer,
# stated rather than hidden.
#
# Exit codes:
#   0  the check ran. `verdict=` says what it found. A cycle is NOT a non-zero
#      exit, and `verdict=empty` is a real answer that reaches 0 like the other
#      two.
#   1  usage error.
#   2  no fusion workbench above the working directory; nothing to compute.
#   3  the plugin's compiled hooks are missing, so nothing could be asked. Its
#      own code and not 2: "there are no live work items" is an answer about the
#      project, and a broken install must never be reported as one.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why this exists, and what it deliberately does not do
#
# `260909-1808_*_may-a-helper-compute-an-order-over-work-items-after-the-portfolio-layer-goes.md`
# (option 3) allows a helper to READ the store and REPORT an order, and stops
# there: no agent asserts a ranking, no marker records one, and the user
# overrides any figure here at will. The portfolio layer that used to rank work
# went at v11 and this is not its return.
#
# It reports and it gates nothing. No exit code carries the verdict, and no
# test, hook or pipeline step runs this program — the stdout-verdict rule every
# other reporting helper in this directory carries, none of them promoted to a
# gate.
#
# It writes nothing. No cache, no index, no marker, no record: the order is
# recomputed per run and discarded, so two runs over an unchanged store print
# the same bytes and neither leaves anything behind for the next one to trust.
#
# The work is hooks/dist/order.js, resolved relative to this script rather than
# through $FUSION_PLUGIN_ROOT, so an install copy and a work tree each run their
# own build. node is already a hard requirement: the plugin's hooks are node
# programs.

set -euo pipefail

here="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
entry="$here/../hooks/dist/order.js"

if [ ! -f "$entry" ]; then
  echo "fusion-work-order: $entry is missing — the plugin's compiled hooks are not installed." >&2
  exit 3
fi

exec node "$entry" "$@"
